The standard approach in .NET is to **wrap file system calls behind an interface** and inject it:

```csharp
public interface IFileSystem
{
    string ReadAllText(string path);
    void WriteAllText(string path, string contents);
    bool DirectoryExists(string path);
}

public class PhysicalFileSystem : IFileSystem
{
    public string ReadAllText(string path) => File.ReadAllText(path);
    public void WriteAllText(string path, string contents) => File.WriteAllText(path, contents);
    public bool DirectoryExists(string path) => Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

Then inject `IFileSystem` into your classes via constructor injection. In tests, mock it or use a simple in-memory implementation.

**Alternatives:**

- **`System.IO.Abstractions`** — a popular NuGet package that provides `IFileSystem` out of the box with an in-memory test helper (`MockFileSystem`). Saves you from writing your own wrapper.
- **`Microsoft.Extensions.FileProviders`** — useful if you only need reads.

The wrapper approach (whether hand-rolled or via `System.IO.Abstractions`) is the most common and well-supported pattern.
